  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Conectando voluntades: fortaleciendo canales de comunicaci&#xf3;n para una pol&#xed;tica fiscal que proteja los DDHH en GUA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The objective of this project focuses on contributing to create the conditions that enable organized civil society to impact fiscal policy. It intentionally takes a step back from aiming directly to impact fiscal policy due to the progressively adverse context that faces Guatemala, which makes almost impossible to influence decision makers at the State level. Specifically, the change (Outcome) that this project pursues is generating new and/or strengthening existing channels of communication between civil society and the State. The project rationalizes that if these institutional channels are not used then these disappear, accelerating the reduction of the civil society space.
&#xa0;
In the last project cycle, ICEFI aimed to influence fiscal policy in Guatemala towards the promotion and defense of human rights. It made progress in this regard, but due to a radical change in context in the middle of the project, it also incorporated a &quot;damage control&quot; approach. It adapted their strategies and mobilized their network of CSOs to act coordinatively to stop tax reforms regressive to human rights promoted by the State.
&#xa0;
This project builds up from the lessons learned in the last project. It explicitly recognizes that this project takes place in a context where in the past two years, independent public investigators and judges are being criminalized, at least 25 of them are in exile, one, a high-level judge specialized in corruption was sentenced to four years in prison. Civil society critical to power is also threatened: the director of a investigative journalist outlet is facing a trial after 9 months of preventive prison, his case was suspiciously elaborated in less than 72 hours, also indigenous communities are being forcibly displaced from their ancestral land and livelihoods by oil palm and mining corporations.</narrative>
  </description>
